Native-wide: keep the margins of the border rows in step with the picture.

Crash 2 draws into 512x216 at y=12 of a 512x240 display (SetDefDrawEnv at
0x80016130). The 12 rows above and below the picture are written only by
fills and uploads, and are black. In native-wide, the side margins of those
rows could turn white and stay white beside the black 4:3 centre: white
corners, top and bottom, after a white flash.

Two ways led there, both fixed in gpu_gl_renderer.c (OpenGL and Direct3D 12):

- The full-screen overlay rect (fades, flashes) has its own wide pass,
  wide_flat_rect_direct. It drew with a full-surface scissor, so it filled
  the margins of every row the rect covered, while the canonical rect stopped
  at the drawing area. Every other mirror pass, and the software reference
  (rt_wide()), keeps to the drawing area's rows; so does this one now.
- The wide surface never follows an upload, and follows a fill only while
  native-wide is active and at a display buffer's base. Whatever else
  reached the border rows' margins stayed there. Each present now copies the
  canonical frame's outermost g_wide_off columns of the displayed rows no
  clipped draw reached since the last present into the two margins,
  wide_fill_border_rows. The rows are tracked per wide surface at hr_begin,
  as the union of the drawing areas used while it was the mirror target. It
  is an unscaled framebuffer copy, like wide_blit_center, so it runs the
  same through the Direct3D 12 layer; it does nothing on a repeated frame or
  when the drawn rows belong to another band. The gl cost JSON counts the
  copies as "border_fills".

tuning/renderer_parity checks it on both APIs ("wide border rows"): stale
white margins, a picture in rows 12-227 and an overlay rect drawn with a
drawing area of rows 100-150. Without either half the check fails.

Native-wide only (Developer mode); 4:3 and squash never run this code. No file
here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; overlay shards are unaffected.

